## Why This Is Asked
The question probes ability to guarantee audit completeness and ordering under load via transactional patterns and robust sinks.

## Key Concepts
- Transactional outbox pattern
- Exactly-once delivery with Kafka
- Idempotent sinks and deduplication
- End-to-end auditing and verification under load

## Code Example
```javascript
async function writeWithOutbox(db, data) {
  const tx = await db.begin();
  await tx.query("UPDATE users SET ... WHERE id = $1", [data.id]);
  await tx.query("INSERT INTO audit_outbox (tx_id, payload) VALUES ($1, $2)", [data.tx_id, JSON.stringify(data.audit)]);
  await tx.commit();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify no audit entries are dropped during a traffic spike?
- Which metrics indicate an outbox backlog or duplicate audits, and how would you address them?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes end-to-end idempotency in CNPA stacks, a realistic beginner-friendly pattern that avoids race conditions. It tests using Redis Lua scripting for atomicity, DB constraints, and retry semantics under load.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys, Redis Lua scripting, TTLs
- PostgreSQL unique constraints, dedup writes
- End-to-end correctness under load, retry semantics
- Exactly-one Kafka publish per unique event

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example API sketch (high level)
async function handle(req, res) {
  const key = req.headers['idempotency-key'] || generateKey();
  const result = await redisEvalCheckSet(key, payload, ttl);
  if (result.dupe) return res.status(200).send(result.value);
  await pg.insert({ event_id: key, payload });
  await kafka.publish({ event_id: key, payload });
  await redisCache(key, { status: 'done' });
  res.status(201).send({ id: key });
}
```

